---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Astro + Datastar">
  <p>
    <code>@wrux/astro-datastar</code> is an Astro integration for
    <a href="https://data-star.dev">Datastar</a> v1.0.2 (vendored bundle — no
    CDN request), plus server helpers for answering Datastar requests from
    Astro endpoints and a minimal <code>data-replace-url</code> plugin.
  </p>

  <h2>Setup</h2>
  <pre><code>// astro.config.mjs
import &lbrace; defineConfig &rbrace; from 'astro/config';
import datastar from '@wrux/astro-datastar';

export default defineConfig(&lbrace;
  integrations: [datastar()],
&rbrace;);</code></pre>

  <p>
    That injects the client entrypoint (the full official plugin set +
    replace-url) into every page. Two options:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>inject: false</code> — keep pages JS-free by default; opt in per
      page with
      <code>&lt;script&gt; import '@wrux/astro-datastar/client'; &lt;/script&gt;</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>entrypoint: '/src/datastar/index.ts'</code> — point at an
      app-local module to control the plugin set. This demo does exactly
      that: its entrypoint loads the package client plus three app-local
      plugins (cloak, collapse, combobox) written against the package's
      <code>engine</code> export.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Datastar gives you</h2>
  <p>
    Reactive <em>signals</em> declared in HTML attributes
    (<code>data-signals</code>, <code>data-bind:*</code>,
    <code>data-text</code>, <code>data-show</code>…) plus backend actions
    (<code>@get</code>, <code>@post</code>…) whose HTML responses are morphed
    into the page by element id. No client framework, no islands — the server
    renders fragments, Datastar patches them in.
  </p>

  <h2>Plugins</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>data-replace-url="&lt;expr&gt;"</code> — ships with the package;
      syncs the address bar to signal state (upstream moved theirs to Pro;
      this is a tiny equivalent).
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>data-collapse</code>, <code>data-cloak</code>,
      <code>data-combobox</code> — defined in this demo app
      (<code>src/datastar/plugins/</code>) as examples of writing your own;
      each demo page shows the plugin source.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Server helpers</h2>
  <pre><code>import &lbrace; readSignals, html &rbrace; from '@wrux/astro-datastar/server';

export const prerender = false;

export const GET: APIRoute = async (&lbrace; request &rbrace;) =&gt; &lbrace;
  const &lbrace; q &rbrace; = await readSignals&lt;&lbrace; q: string &rbrace;&gt;(request);
  return html(`&lt;ul id="results"&gt;…&lt;/ul&gt;`);
&rbrace;;</code></pre>
  <p>
    For anything a plain HTML morph can't express — patching signals,
    append/remove modes, streaming — build SSE events with
    <code>patchElements()</code> / <code>patchSignals()</code> /
    <code>removeElements()</code> and answer with <code>sse(…)</code> or a
    long-lived <code>sseStream(run)</code>. See the
    <a href="/examples/stream">streaming example</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="hint">Pick an example from the sidebar — each shows its source.</p>
</Layout>
